### What is instanceof and pattern matching?

instanceof checks if an object is of a particular type at runtime. Pattern matching eliminates the redundant cast.

### How did it work before Java 16?

```java
public void processAccount(Account account) {
    if (account instanceof SavingsAccount) {
        SavingsAccount sa = (SavingsAccount) account; // explicit cast: redundant
        java.math.BigDecimal interest = sa.calculateInterest();
        sa.deposit(interest);
    } else if (account instanceof CurrentAccount) {
        CurrentAccount ca = (CurrentAccount) account; // another explicit cast
        ca.checkOverdraft();
    }
    // Problems: redundant (just checked instanceof), error-prone (copy-paste wrong cast), verbose.
}
```

### How does Java 16+ pattern matching work?

```java
public void processAccount(Account account) {
    if (account instanceof SavingsAccount sa) {
        // sa is already cast to SavingsAccount: no explicit cast
        java.math.BigDecimal interest = sa.calculateInterest();
        sa.deposit(interest);
    } else if (account instanceof CurrentAccount ca) {
        ca.checkOverdraft();
    }
    // One expression does check + cast + bind. Cleaner. Safer. Shorter.
}
```

### How does flow scoping work?

```java
// Inside if-block: sa is in scope
if (account instanceof SavingsAccount sa) {
    sa.calculateInterest(); // valid: type proven by instanceof
}
// sa is NOT available here: out of scope

// With && guard: sa is in scope for the right side
if (account instanceof SavingsAccount sa && sa.getBalance().compareTo(threshold) > 0) {
    sa.calculateInterest(); // valid: instanceof passed, && guarantees sa is bound
}

// With || : sa is NOT in scope (type not proven)
// if (account instanceof SavingsAccount sa || sa.getBalance()...) // compile error

// Negated condition: sa available after early return
if (!(account instanceof SavingsAccount sa)) {
    return; // exit: instanceof failed
}
sa.calculateInterest(); // valid: if we reached here, instanceof passed
```

### How does Java 21 switch pattern matching work?

```java
public String describeAccount(Account account) {
    return switch (account) {
        case SavingsAccount sa when sa.getBalance().compareTo(new java.math.BigDecimal("100000")) > 0
            -> "Premium Savings: " + sa.getAccountId();
        case SavingsAccount sa
            -> "Regular Savings: " + sa.getAccountId();
        case CurrentAccount ca
            -> "Current: " + ca.getAccountId();
        case null
            -> "No account provided";
        default
            -> "Unknown account type";
    };
    // Each case: type check + cast + bind + optional guard (when clause)
}
```

### How does instanceof handle null?

```java
Account account = null;
boolean result = account instanceof SavingsAccount; // false: null-safe
// No NullPointerException. null instanceof AnyType is always false.
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using || with pattern matching, which does not guarantee the type.

```java
// WRONG: || does not guarantee sa is bound
// if (account instanceof SavingsAccount sa || sa.getBalance() > 0) // compile error

// CORRECT: use && (type guaranteed for right side)
if (account instanceof SavingsAccount sa && sa.getBalance().signum() > 0) { /* valid */ }
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Pattern matching in switch with sealed classes enables exhaustive handling: no default needed, and adding a new subtype causes compile errors.
- Record patterns (Java 21) enable destructuring: `case Trade(String id, BigDecimal amount)` extracts fields directly.
- In Spring, pattern matching is used in exception handlers and event processors to handle polymorphic hierarchies concisely.
